<!--
  MCU session-tx buffer-pool config: the SSOT for the slots an lwIP (MCU)
  node encodes outbound session frames into, and that an Ethernet MAC's
  transmit DMA then reads in place (ARCHITECTURE section 9.1: "Codec::encode
  writes directly into a pool slot ... DMA controller reads slot via
  pre-built descriptor"; section 9.4 places TX pools beside the RX pools).

  Born with its consumer (the reassembly_pool pattern): `wz-link-lwip`'s
  `tx_pool` module, behind that crate's `tx-pool` feature. A slot is lent to
  the session as the memory of one lwIP pbuf, the session writes the frame
  into it, lwIP writes the UDP, IPv4 and Ethernet headers in front of it IN
  the slot, and a MAC that gathers in place is handed the frame at the
  slot's own address. The generated lifecycle is what governs the slot:
  acquired for encode when it is lent, armed for transmit when it is sent,
  started when a MAC queues it to be read in place, complete when the MAC
  reports it done, and un-armed and returned when the frame left by a copy
  or never reached a MAC.

  Values (and their derivations):
    - slot_size 1536: the slot holds the pbuf, not only the bytes. lwIP's
      custom pbuf record (struct pbuf_custom: the 16-byte struct pbuf and
      one function pointer, 20 bytes on a 32-bit target) sits at the start
      of the slot, then the room lwIP keeps in front of a transport-layer
      payload (PBUF_TRANSPORT, 14 + 20 + 8 = 42 bytes, 44 at MEM_ALIGNMENT
      4), then the payload. 1536 - 20 - 44 = 1472, which is the largest UDP
      payload a 1500-byte Ethernet MTU carries unfragmented. 1536 is also a
      multiple of the alignment below, and the CYT4BF MAC's own buffer
      length. On a 64-bit host the record is larger and a slot carries
      less; a frame that does not fit a slot is encoded on the heap, as
      with any lend.
    - slot_count 8: a slot is out from the lend until the MAC reports its
      frame done, and the lwIP shim lets a MAC read at most 8 frames in
      place at once (WZ_ETHIF_HELD_MAX in lwip-sys/shim.c). A dry pool is
      not an error: the lend falls back to a pbuf from lwIP's own heap,
      which is the path before this pool existed. 8 x 1536 = 12 KiB.

  cache-policy is `non-cacheable`, which is a statement about where the
  firmware puts the pool: the DMA master reads a slot behind the CPU's
  back, so the pool is placed in memory the MPU marks non-cacheable (on the
  Zephyr T2G image, Zephyr's `.nocache` section, where the MAC's own
  descriptor rings already live), and no cache maintenance is emitted or
  needed. That section is NOLOAD and the generated Rust pool owns its
  storage inline, so `tx_pool::install` writes the whole pool before it is
  used (a pool of zeroes is a pool of free slots). A board that cannot
  place it so must not read the slots in place: the CYT4BF MAC reads in
  place only inside the window its board names.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       xmlns:sce="http://sce.dev/ext"
       sce:kind="buffer-pool" name="session_tx_pool_mcu" version="1.0">
  <sce:slot-count>8</sce:slot-count>
  <sce:slot-size>1536</sce:slot-size>
  <sce:section>sram1</sce:section>
  <sce:alignment>32</sce:alignment>
  <sce:cache-policy>non-cacheable</sce:cache-policy>
</scxml>
